WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.020
On October 7th, Brazilian voters went to
the polls to choose a new president

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:12.360
and Congress — in one of the most
complicated and polarizing elections in

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.100
that country's history. Jair Bolsonaro
and Fernando Haddad have advanced to

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.860
the second round. Bolsonaro's strong
finish — falling just short of a

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:25.800
first round victory — stunned the country's
political class. His tough talk resonated

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.099
with many voters, who are fed up with
widespread political corruption and

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:34.500
staggering crime rates. Haddad is the
stand-in for former president Lula da Silva.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.900
Although his big-government message
resonates with the country's poor,

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.600
the Workers' Party took a
beating in the first round.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:45.140
Economic growth is sluggish. Corruption
scandals have fueled cynicism.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:50.160
Murder rates six-times that of the United States
are a security crisis. And this campaign has

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.350
polarized public opinion. Bolsonaro
has the edge, but Haddad can appeal to

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:59.240
millions of voters who did not
participate in the first round.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.740
Both will try to make the case before the
October 28th runoff that he's the man

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.640
who can put Brazil's house in order.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:10.920
What does the future hold for Brazil?
Let us know in your comments

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.060
and let us know what other topics you'd like
our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:20.260
and be sure to LIKE and subscribe for
more research and videos from AEI.

